#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519671} :activity/id #uuid "e7eca05f-e99e-590a-bf49-64802c41086f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049575 :topic/id #uuid "01533816-c819-5cab-8910-7cf6afa63383" :topic/math-academy-id 6685} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d6581fb-1cd9-5f47-8ff9-5979ac885085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450440} {:answer/id #uuid "523d5a9b-bdc7-5943-9f2e-bd9519abc022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450441}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450440} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b68c3fb-619e-551d-af87-e5d8bc615d9c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451174} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "883b44a2-bf01-5f77-a3ef-4667d3673879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450442} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d407e9-bdde-51d4-838c-250cb8be5b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450443}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450443} :answer-field/id #uuid "40e6b0e4-794c-511c-a8b6-8176c4d57f20" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451175} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b751b7e-6ae0-51fb-a6ba-d823ccb81759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450444} {:answer/id #uuid "f0455e55-18d0-5a46-be37-2c94e8c15bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450445}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450445} :answer-field/id #uuid "89bd6593-a175-58b8-8df8-17cd965f7378" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451176} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d9c5801-9019-57ca-93c4-8d5e18dac042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450446} {:answer/id #uuid "03fa487c-d0a5-5d09-b69c-f51e9fd595b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450447}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450446} :answer-field/id #uuid "70e87aa0-6c37-57e0-9829-c738cc1d85a5" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "55adb6e6-36a2-5272-9189-7161d38a92cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-333578" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the trapezoidal method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=2,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =7,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The stability function is $R(7Δx)=\\frac{1+7Δx/2}{1-7Δx/2}.$\n- **II.** The stability region is $|\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}|<1.$\n- **III.** The method is absolutely stable.\n- **IV.** The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.5.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=2,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the trapezoidal method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-7y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the trapezoidal method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the trapezoidal method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-7{y}_{n}+(-7{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\frac{7}{2}{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{7}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\ (1+\\frac{7}{2}Δx){y}_{n+1} & =(1-\\frac{7}{2}Δx){y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(7Δx)=\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}.$ Hence, statement I is false.\n\n- The stability region of the method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(7Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Rearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|2-7Δx|}{|2+7Δx|} & <1 \\\\ |2-7Δx| & <|2+7Δx| \\\\ (2-7Δx)^{2} & <(2+7Δx)^{2} \\\\ 4-28Δx+49(Δx)^{2} & <4+28Δx+49(Δx)^{2} \\\\ -28Δx & <28Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Δx>0,$ this inequality is always true. Thus, the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, since it is absolutely stable, the trapezoidal method is stable for step size $Δx=0.5,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "18bfeff9-da8e-5adf-b0b0-f3f134960c7b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519633}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519590 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a83fe5b-8074-5c2d-93ca-132b209dd66e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519009} {:answer/id #uuid "675fbddc-fb55-57b4-97c1-275b6152cefd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519143} {:answer/id #uuid "d4cea126-5b1a-5675-bae4-21fe72227319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519342} {:answer/id #uuid "da511c1c-87de-5875-ad76-4fd6abdb409d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519351} {:answer/id #uuid "df235f97-7d7c-5f76-8fe1-ed5b46b28e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519360}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519143} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7ebb238-bf1c-5419-8e2b-8b67c38f85fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e4d56835-15e1-5dd9-b189-b250c69cae71" :question/math-academy-id "q-334717" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the modified Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.3$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1.1$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=1.7$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=2.9$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the modified Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the modified Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the modified Euler method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.3)| & =|1-0.3+\\frac{(0.3)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{149}{200}| \\\\  & =0.745<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.1)| & =|1-1.1+\\frac{(1.1)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{101}{200}| \\\\  & =0.505<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.7)| & =|1-1.7+\\frac{(1.7)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{149}{200}| \\\\  & =0.745<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.9)| & =|1-2.9+\\frac{(2.9)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{461}{200}| \\\\  & =2.305>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the modified Euler method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the modified Euler method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2,$ so it is not absolutely stable."} :step/id #uuid "236a5063-a320-5c79-a165-40f007e87d4e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519669}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308543 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4e6a2e4-a130-5f3a-b5af-59edc314f0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466125} {:answer/id #uuid "999a1a3e-f07f-51c1-89c7-2c2e5ce92eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466126}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466126} :answer-field/id #uuid "29ab3db8-e08d-5ad8-a06f-cc382521ee79" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466906} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b7b5bcd-cddb-55f0-b269-79a8ab77d160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466127} {:answer/id #uuid "fdaf88bc-383d-5594-a096-e0a34b4de52c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466128}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466127} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb7bad4e-048c-587a-a7df-e955747cbe46" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466907} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "119754ec-a866-5ea0-9294-1804c408bd5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466129} {:answer/id #uuid "97a0407f-8581-544f-91ac-3d5c1b386e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466130}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466130} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa4ce22c-631f-5997-9059-e3bcdc6038d9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466908} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2866b660-be68-5c27-a884-63d993e90ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466131} {:answer/id #uuid "a67b55b5-3a28-54fb-995f-718d03c297f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466132}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466132} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbb60801-a619-5246-8b60-f108f387db98" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7c9db1fa-3df6-5592-9112-986f10641d50" :question/math-academy-id "q-334014" :question/problem "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **unstable**, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n- **I.** $Δx=\\frac{2.2}{\\lambda }$\n- **II.** $Δx=\\frac{0.4}{\\lambda }$\n- **III.** $Δx=\\frac{2}{\\lambda }$\n- **IV.** $Δx=\\frac{5}{\\lambda }$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nNow, we consider each step size in turn.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2.2}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2.2\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls outside the given region, Euler's method is not stable (or unstable) for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{0.4}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=0.4.$ Since this value falls within the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <\\lambda Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <0.4<2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method must be stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls outside the given region, Euler's method is not stable (or unstable) for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=5\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls outside the given region, Euler's method is not stable (or unstable) for this step size.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"."} :step/id #uuid "50ccc479-0bdf-5248-b544-a38b78cc03a5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519622}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519669 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcfddc24-c58f-5c4f-84e9-ef551982185b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471427} {:answer/id #uuid "9769274f-fb36-5fbc-8541-fc90883166a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471428}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471428} :answer-field/id #uuid "41a7fbf7-0d41-5041-aae1-a39e147b182f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472219} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f185f3b-0a68-5abd-a8c2-38bf014f58b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471429} {:answer/id #uuid "fa8a98cb-e1a9-56a3-92c7-055782d9153d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471430}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471430} :answer-field/id #uuid "74f644d8-2edd-5b0b-937d-f2bfddc4567b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472220} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088c5cde-2edc-582f-8a1e-928e3b8226d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471431} {:answer/id #uuid "dfda1d96-e20f-55b1-8aa9-981ec2a0f203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471432}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471431} :answer-field/id #uuid "79254c88-eae6-5edf-bb54-1cc11619fc3f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472221} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "753fff6f-e13f-5c5d-ae71-087ae88e049e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471433} {:answer/id #uuid "e59e1c38-8632-55cd-89ae-49999007c409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471434}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471434} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b3380c0-50d4-5961-9f40-7aaca9597d4f" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5ea2a49c-9462-54d3-b8e9-50d1002b7880" :question/math-academy-id "q-334775" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the modified Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n- **I.** $\\lambda Δx=0.65$\n- **II.** $\\lambda Δx=1.35$\n- **III.** $\\lambda Δx=3.6$\n- **IV.** $\\lambda Δx=1.25$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the modified Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the modified Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the modified Euler method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.65,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.65)| & =|1-0.65+\\frac{(0.65)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{449}{800}| \\\\  & =0.561\\,25<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.35,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.35)| & =|1-1.35+\\frac{(1.35)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{449}{800}| \\\\  & =0.561\\,25<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3.6)| & =|1-3.6+\\frac{(3.6)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{97}{25}| \\\\  & =3.88>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the modified Euler method is unstable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.25)| & =|1-1.25+\\frac{(1.25)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{17}{32}| \\\\  & =0.531\\,25<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Since this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the modified Euler method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2,$ so it is not absolutely stable."} :step/id #uuid "f438fdd3-89dd-5c2a-9c44-3d2afb1adf2d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519671 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "554a3f25-c6d4-56f1-97a1-e72f6e996868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450424} {:answer/id #uuid "1bf7d503-a537-5980-a459-e3d845c2a7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450425}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450425} :answer-field/id #uuid "2668b372-5058-5bcf-87d1-b3dc18aa9e3e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451166} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1d305cf-a373-552c-84d7-e16b441a5d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450426} {:answer/id #uuid "20b79c3e-cfe8-5182-8d84-c0226c536bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450427}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450427} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc9d7e34-4fe0-5a4b-be27-0a630b6ca736" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451167} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a745d4d-5653-50dd-8c45-7098cb48f439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450428} {:answer/id #uuid "05afd7a7-372e-5f9d-8fe6-0eaf11538d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450429}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450429} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6caa9e2-7fc4-57e0-84db-0278682927f4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451168} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75636e46-7f14-5059-a314-ce331ac05304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450430} {:answer/id #uuid "2a562ffb-8f60-5832-8dc2-4814364ea999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450431}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450430} :answer-field/id #uuid "e64ab8c0-b38a-58c0-8fc6-2259dd4a7a0d" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6a2ae136-3885-5a0c-a306-c58f92aeffc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-333572" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the trapezoidal method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=2,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =5,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The stability function is $R(5Δx)=\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}.$\n- **II.** The stability region is $|\\frac{2-5Δx}{2+5Δx}|<1.$\n- **III.** The method is absolutely stable.\n- **IV.** The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=1.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=2,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the trapezoidal method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the trapezoidal method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the trapezoidal method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-5{y}_{n}+(-5{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\ (1+\\frac{5}{2}Δx){y}_{n+1} & =(1-\\frac{5}{2}Δx){y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(5Δx)=\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The stability region of the method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(5Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{2-5Δx}{2+5Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Rearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|2-5Δx|}{|2+5Δx|} & <1 \\\\ |2-5Δx| & <|2+5Δx| \\\\ (2-5Δx)^{2} & <(2+5Δx)^{2} \\\\ 4-20Δx+25(Δx)^{2} & <4+20Δx+25(Δx)^{2} \\\\ -20Δx & <20Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Δx>0,$ this inequality is always true. Thus, the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, since it is absolutely stable, the trapezoidal method is stable for step size $Δx=1,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "fd7e31ca-0961-5f9c-aed7-3bd1d98457f3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519614}}] :activity/title "Stability of Numerical Methods" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518989}]}
